{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHvmevLsYhTAipBn2Hgb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nothing happens to the test tomorrow. You're right that `sync.sh` rewrites `tracker.json` every hour, which is why the test doesn't read that file. It keeps its own copy of today's 5 issues (3 open), writes them to a temporary file, and calls `weekly_report()` on that file. I just ran both tests in a scratch directory with no `tracker.json`, and both passed. So when the sync changes the real data, the test still passes.\n\nThe trade-off is what the test proves. It shows that `weekly_report()` counts open issues correctly. It does not check tomorrow's real `tracker.json`. If the live export changes its format, for example a new state like `\"Open\"` or `\"reopened\"`, this test will not catch it.\n\nIf you want the live file checked too, there are two options:\n\n1. **Keep it as is (my default).** A stable test of the counting logic.\n2. **Add a check on the live file's format.** For example, a test that every issue in the real `tracker.json` has a `state` of `\"open\"` or `\"closed\"`. It would not hardcode a count, so it would only fail when the data's shape changes. It also ties the test suite to whatever the sync wrote last.\n\nA test that expects exactly 3 from the live file is the option to avoid: it would break the first time someone opens or closes an issue."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":623,"cache_read_input_tokens":40506,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":623},"output_tokens":421,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"689b526f-5ed0-44d4-b193-3b2d8818fc13","uuid":"6be80649-c9b4-4d58-902a-5269b907663e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T20:44:35.410Z